Selection (docs/01-engine.md "wire up perft for 2-3 candidates, pick one"),
measured on this box, same 5-position suite, bulk-counted, single-threaded,
-O3 -march=native, 442,133,692 nodes total:

  chess-library  0.78 s   569 Mn/s
  surge          0.85 s   522 Mn/s   (nkarve/surge @ 184e59f)

Speed was a tie; API friction decided it. surge does not compile on GCC 11
without patches (`extern constexpr` definitions, a missing <cstring>, and an
`extern inline` scheme that only links as a single translation unit), templates
the side to move as a compile-time parameter — every runtime call site needs its
own dispatch — and ships no FEN output, no Zobrist-based repetition, and no draw
detection. chess-library gives us all of those plus SAN/UCI I/O in one header.
Cross-check beyond the suite: startpos depth 7 = 3,195,901,860, exact.

Vendored as a single header rather than a submodule: it is one self-contained
file, and pinning the exact bytes we perft-validated matters more here than
easy upstream pulls. To update: drop in the new chess.hpp, bump the commit
above, and re-run tests/run_perft.sh — the full suite must stay exact.
